<p>Above, we encountered an issue. We needed to color node 4 blue, but one =
of node 4=E2=80=99s neighbors, node 0, is also colored blue. This means the=
 graph cannot be colored using our graph coloring strategy. In other words,=
 the graph is not bipartite.</p>
<hr>
<p>We now have a strategy to confirm if a graph is bipartite using the two-=
coloring technique. But how can we be sure that it always works? We=E2=80=
=99ve been coloring adjacent nodes in alternating colors from the beginning=
 of the DFS. This means if we encounter a situation where two adjacent node=
s are the same color, it means there=E2=80=99s no way to color the graph di=
fferently, since we=E2=80=99ve been following the rule of using different c=
olors for neighboring nodes all along.</p>
<p><strong>Handling multiple components</strong><br>
Keep in mind the input isn't necessarily a graph that's fully connected. It=
 could be a graph with multiple components, such as this:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
